\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 1. Abstract\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 This case study examines the role of operating system functions in a hospital management system where multiple departments must handle patient data, doctor schedules, patient admission flows, billing, inventory, and lab records concurrently. Efficient CPU scheduling, process synchronization, memory management, and deadlock prevention are crucial to ensure timely and reliable healthcare operations. The project demonstrates how OS concepts can be applied to improve responsiveness, manage resources effectively, and maintain service continuity in a real-life hospital environment.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 2. Introduction and Real-Life Background\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 Modern hospitals operate with interconnected digital systems that involve patient registration, admissions, appointments, emergency response, pharmacy management, laboratory data, and billing. As multiple processes run simultaneously, waiting times and resource contention may occur. Operating systems provide a structured way to allocate CPU time, memory, and devices while ensuring fairness, correctness, and system stability. The Travel Booking Data Organiser repository is used as a practical software reference for understanding how structured dashboards, event handling, and workflow automation can be mapped to an OS-driven healthcare scheduling and resource management problem.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 3. Problem Statement\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 In a hospital environment, multiple users and departments request services simultaneously. Without intelligent scheduling and resource management, patients may face delays, resources may be underutilized, and deadlocks may occur in system processes such as patient records, billing modules, pharmacy access, and lab results. The challenge is to design an operating system-based solution that prioritizes critical healthcare tasks, balances load, and prevents system bottlenecks.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 4. Objectives of the Case Study\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 - To analyze resource contention in a hospital workflow.\par
- To explain the application of CPU scheduling algorithms and process management in healthcare systems.\par
- To demonstrate synchronization, deadlock prevention, and memory techniques in a realistic scenario.\par
- To propose an OS-based system design for efficient hospital operations.\par
- To relate the case study to program outcomes and course objectives.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 5. Operating System Concepts Involved\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 The case study involves major operating system themes such as:\par
- Process scheduling and priority-based execution\par
- CPU scheduling algorithms such as FCFS, SJF, Round Robin, and Priority Scheduling\par
- Process synchronization for concurrent hospital workflows\par
- Deadlock detection and avoidance in shared resources\par
- Memory management for patient data and records\par
- Input-output management for lab, pharmacy, and billing activities\par
- Resource allocation and fairness among departments\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 6. Proposed Methodology and Algorithms\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 A hospital workflow model can be represented as a set of processes such as patient registration, appointment handling, billing, inventory update, and lab result processing. Each process requires CPU time and shared resources. A priority-based scheduling algorithm can be used so that emergency care and critical patient tasks are processed first. Synchronization tools like semaphores can protect shared data such as patient records and inventory. Deadlock prevention can be ensured by ordering resource acquisition and limiting exclusive lock conditions. Memory allocation can be optimized by using dynamic memory strategies and paging for large patient datasets.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 7. Program Code or Simulation\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 A sample simulation may include process tables, arrival time, burst time, waiting time, turnaround time, and average waiting time. The system may implement a Round Robin or Priority Scheduling algorithm to allocate CPU time to multiple hospital tasks. Pseudocode or Python code can be used to compare algorithms and assess performance.\par
Example:\par
- Process P1: Admission and patient intake\par
- Process P2: Emergency consultation\par
- Process P3: Billing and insurance update\par
- Process P4: Lab report generation\par
- Process P5: Pharmacy stock update\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 8. Sample Input and Output\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 Sample input could include process ID, arrival time, burst time, and priority values for several hospital services. The output may show Gantt chart, waiting time, turnaround time, and throughput. A typical result may indicate that priority scheduling reduces waiting time for emergency services while round robin keeps fairness across departments.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 9. Results and Analysis\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 The results show that process scheduling directly affects patient service quality and hospital efficiency. Emergency patient tasks complete faster under priority scheduling, while batch tasks are processed fairly under round robin. Synchronization avoids data corruption in shared patient records and billing modules. Deadlock prevention ensures smooth service delivery during simultaneous requests. Overall, the operating system model improves system responsiveness and resource utilization.\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs28\b 10. Engineering Constraints and Sustainability Considerations\par
\pard\plain\f1\fs22\b0 This system must respect constraints such as real-time response, data security, limited computational resources, and reliability. The application should support multiple users without data loss, preserve patient privacy, and be scalable for growing hospital demand. Sustainability is improved by reducing waiting time, efficient resource use, and automation of routine healthcare tasks. The design also aligns with green and efficient computing by minimizing unnecessary CPU cycles and reducing operational delays.\par
